# The `.agent/` convention

Every repo gets one agent-owned directory. Nothing an agent produces goes
anywhere else without you asking for it.

```
.agent/
  notes/       synthesized notes and their dated archive
  research/    research memos
  plans/       implementation plans
  diagrams/    generated diagrams
  profiles/    per-repo bindings of global skills
```

Add `.agent/` to `.gitignore`. It is scratch space and telemetry, not source.

## Why this exists

Agent output used to land wherever the agent felt like putting it — root-level
markdown, ad-hoc folders, files you did not ask for. That sprawl is what makes
a monorepo unnavigable. One directory, gitignored, ends it.

The containment gate enforces this: new markdown outside `.agent/`, `docs/`,
or a pre-existing docs path is denied.

## Writing outside `.agent/`

Allowed without ceremony:

- Editing files that already exist.
- `README.md` and anything already under `docs/`.
- Any path the user names explicitly.

Everything else that is *new* markdown belongs in `.agent/`.

## Profiles: binding a global skill to one repo

Global skills carry the routine. Profiles carry the parameters. This is how a
skill stays portable (no workspace paths in its body) while still being
concrete in the repo where it runs.

A profile lives at `.agent/profiles/<skill-name>.md` and is a defaults table,
not a reimplementation:

```markdown
# ghsa-triage — acme/widget

| Param | Value |
|-------|-------|
| repo | acme/widget |
| repo_path | . |
| security_context | Node service, Postgres, no direct SQL |

## Repo notes

- The ORM layer wraps all queries; raw SQL only in `src/legacy/`.
- Advisories against `src/legacy/` are automatically higher severity.
```

Rules for profiles:

- **Never restate the routine.** If a profile explains *how* to do the work,
  the global skill is underspecified — fix the skill instead.
- **Parameters and repo-specific context only.**
- A profile that grows past a screen is a signal the skill needs a real
  parameter, not more prose.

Skills read their profile first, before applying defaults. The session-start
gate lists the profiles present in the current repo, so you can see which
skills are already bound here.
